implementationPlan.note:
Tie-break replaced. Step-1 red went green with no other test touched: 23 passed (23), exit 0.

WAS:
  const byCode = a.code < b.code ? -1 : a.code > b.code ? 1 : 0;
  return byCode !== 0 ? byCode : String(a.edgeKey).localeCompare(String(b.edgeKey));
NOW:
  const byCode = a.code < b.code ? -1 : a.code > b.code ? 1 : 0;
  if (byCode !== 0) {
    return byCode;
  }
  // Code units, never localeCompare: collation depends on the host's ICU data,
  // so two machines would order the same reasons differently - and that failure
  // never reproduces locally. Same form as the code tie-break directly above.
  const edgeA = String(a.edgeKey);
  const edgeB = String(b.edgeKey);
  return edgeA < edgeB ? -1 : edgeA > edgeB ? 1 : 0;

CONVENTION FOLLOWED, NOT INVENTED (task rail 3). This is the scheduler's own inline idiom, already at admission/admission-model.ts:165 and readiness/readiness-cursor.ts:60, and identical in form to :151 immediately above — the comparator is now internally consistent instead of using two different comparison disciplines two lines apart. No fourth helper introduced, and byCodeUnit was NOT imported from @moe/import: its doc comment scopes it as "The ONLY string comparator this package may use", and crossing a package boundary for a three-line comparator would require a real dependency edge (manifest, lockfile importer, compiled probe) under the global dependency-edge rail.

TWO DELIBERATE CHOICES INSIDE THE FIX:
1. Early return for byCode instead of extending the nested ternary. The nested form would have been `byCode !== 0 ? byCode : edgeA < edgeB ? -1 : ...`, a three-level ternary; the early return matches the `if (byLayer !== 0) { return byLayer; }` shape already used at :148-150 in this same comparator.
2. Named locals rather than destructuring. I first wrote `const [x, y] = [String(a.edgeKey), String(b.edgeKey)]`, then changed it: that allocates an array on EVERY comparison inside a sort, and x/y read poorly beside the comparator's own a/b. edgeA/edgeB allocate nothing and say what they are. One line more; still inside budget.

SCOPE HELD (task rail 2). READINESS_LAYERS primary ordering at :147 and the code tie-break at :151 are byte-identical to before. Only the edgeKey level changed. The public ordering contract is unchanged — this makes the ORDER host-independent, it does not redefine it.

LINE BUDGET: 248 physical lines by grep -c '' (was 240), under the 250 target with 2 lines of headroom. PowerShell Measure-Object -Line undercounts and was not used. The plan flagged this file as tight; the fix stayed a replacement plus a three-line comment rather than adding a helper function, which is what kept it under.

The comment is not decoration — it is the fourth site in the repo carrying this convention, and canonical-bytes.ts:57-64 explains why it matters: a green suite on one host is exactly what this defect looks like.
